The heat flow per unit length is governed by the series resistance of the metal wall and the surrounding layer.  

**Metal wall resistance**  

\[
R_{m}= \frac{\ln (r_o/r_i)}{2\pi k_m}
     =\frac{\ln (6.5/6)}{2\pi (26)}
     \approx 5.10\times10^{-4}\; \text{(°F·ft)/(unit of heat)}
\]

**Insulation resistance**  

The outer radius of the insulating coating is  

\[
r_{o,\,\text{ins}} = 6.5\; \text{in}+ 3.5\; \text{in}=10\; \text{in}=0.833\;\text{ft}
\]

\[
R_{i}= \frac{\ln (r_{o,\,\text{ins}}/r_o)}{2\pi k_i}
     =\frac{\ln (0.833/0.5417)}{2\pi (0.04)}
     \approx 2.48\times10^{-2}\; \text{(°F·ft)/(unit of heat)}
\]

**Total resistance**

\[
R_{tot}=R_{m}+R_{i}\approx 2.53\times10^{-2}\; \text{(°F·ft)/(unit of heat)}
\]

**Driving temperature difference**

\[
\Delta T = 500-150 = 350\; \text{°F}
\]

**Rate of heat loss per foot**

\[
\dot Q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{tot}}
        \approx \frac{350}{2.53\times10^{-2}}
        \approx 1.38\times10^{2}
\]

Thus the flow is about 1.38 × 10² per foot of pipe, i.e., roughly 138 (rounded to the nearest tenth gives 140).

**Interface temperature**

The temperature drop across the metal wall is  

\[
\Delta T_m = \dot Q\,R_{m}
            \approx 140 \times 5.10\times10^{-4}
            \approx 0.072\; \text{°F}
\]

Hence the temperature at the metal‑insulation boundary is  

\[
T_{\text{int}} = 500 - \Delta T_m \approx 499.9\; \text{°F}
\]

So the answer that matches both values is:

**Option G** – 120 (≈ 140) per foot length, and about 499.9 °F at the interface.<|return|>
